---
import "@/styles/article.css";

import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import SectionHeader from "@/components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import { PROFILE_NAME } from "@/lib/site";

const title = `A Personal Trust Control Plane | ${PROFILE_NAME}`;
const description =
  "The current design for verifiable, monitorable, and recoverable public identity releases.";

const VERIFY_RELEASE = `# Fetch the deployed release metadata and identity manifest
curl -sO https://yusuke-hayashi.com/.well-known/release.json
curl -sO https://yusuke-hayashi.com/.well-known/identity.json
SOURCE_COMMIT="$(node -p 'JSON.parse(require("fs").readFileSync("release.json")).source.commit')"

# Accept provenance only from the exact release authority
gh attestation verify identity.json \\
  --repo yhay81/profile \\
  --signer-workflow yhay81/profile/.github/workflows/cloudflare.yml \\
  --cert-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \\
  --source-ref refs/heads/main \\
  --source-digest "$SOURCE_COMMIT" \\
  --predicate-type https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1 \\
  --deny-self-hosted-runners`;
---

<Base description={description} path="/trust-control-plane" title={title}>
  <main>
    <section>
      <SectionHeader
        label="Article"
        no="CURRENT"
        title="A Personal Trust Control Plane"
      />

      <p class="lead">
        How I make public identity releases verifiable, monitorable, and
        recoverable without treating one platform or one long-lived key as the
        whole system. A clearsigned copy is available at <a
          href="/trust-control-plane.txt.asc">/trust-control-plane.txt.asc</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">The trust problem</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Public identity is distributed across a domain, GitHub, email, an
        Ethereum account, ENS, Nostr, Bluesky, and other services. No single
        badge or account proves control of all of them, and a compromised host
        can serve a convincing but false set of files.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">The system answers four concrete questions:</p>

      <ol class="numberList">
        <li>What is the current canonical identity state?</li>
        <li>Which workflow and source commit produced the release?</li>
        <li>Which proofs bind each public identifier to the subject?</li>
        <li>How is authority stopped or recovered after compromise?</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="heading">Control plane and distribution plane</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        A private control-plane repository holds expected public values,
        policies, schemas, pre-publication artifacts, verification code, and
        runbooks. It contains no private keys, seeds, passwords, recovery codes,
        or physical locations.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        A public repository and this website form the distribution plane. They
        publish the manifest, deterministic release archive, verifier, schemas,
        proofs, audit history, and compatibility key material. The public site
        is rebuildable and cannot redefine the verifier's pinned expectations by
        itself.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Sigstore-first releases</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Routine releases use GitHub Actions OIDC, a short-lived Sigstore
        certificate, and SLSA provenance. The workflow attests the identity
        manifest, a deterministic identity release archive, and the complete
        deployment archive.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Verification pins the OIDC issuer, repository, signer workflow, source
        ref, source commit, SLSA predicate, hosted-runner requirement, and
        artifact digest. An attestation is not accepted merely because its
        cryptographic signature is valid. Pull-request builds have read-only
        permissions and cannot request an OIDC signing identity.
      </p>

      <CodeBlock code={VERIFY_RELEASE} wrap />

      <h2 class="heading">Machine-readable current state</h2>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          <a href="/.well-known/identity.json"
            ><code class="inlineCode">/.well-known/identity.json</code></a
          > names the current identifiers, validity window, verifier hash, audit-history
          hash, and proof registry.
        </li>
        <li>
          The deterministic identity archive packages the exact public materials
          required for independent verification.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/.well-known/release.json"
            ><code class="inlineCode">/.well-known/release.json</code></a
          > names the deployed source commit.
        </li>
        <li>
          The manifest also carries an OpenPGP detached signature for WKD,
          <code class="inlineCode">security.txt</code>, cross-proofs, and
          configured Git-signature compatibility. It is not the primary proof of
          routine release provenance.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading">Authority and recovery</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The release plane is separate from permanent identity continuity. An
        offline authority policy supports three hardware-backed public keys with
        a threshold of two. It is reserved for changing the accepted release
        authority, rotating root keys, and recovery; it does not sign every
        routine release.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The offline root is not configured. It becomes active only after real
        hardware keys, an out-of-band policy digest, two valid approvals, and a
        recovery drill exist. An unperformed ceremony is reported as an
        incomplete capability.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Identifier adapters</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The current Ethereum/OpenPGP cross-proof and ENS records bind the public
        account to the other compatibility identifiers. The verifier also
        supports authority-bound EIP-712 proofs for EOAs and ERC-1271 proofs for
        smart accounts; that proof remains unpublished while the offline
        authority root is unconfigured. ENS is a name-resolution adapter, not a
        foundation. No adapter can replace the release authority policy.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Audit evidence without stale documentation</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Normative documents and tool help describe only the current system.
        Cryptographic audit evidence remains separate and append-only:
        <code class="inlineCode">identity-history.json</code>, content-addressed
        checkpoints, signed Git commits, and release attestations. Current
        instructions stay concise without silently deleting earlier signed
        states.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Monitoring</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The production verifier checks manifest integrity, compatibility
        signatures, identifier bindings, expiry, DNS, WKD, ENS, Git signatures,
        third-party attestations, and audit-history invariants. A separate
        verifier checks Sigstore provenance for the manifest and release
        archive.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        GitHub Actions runs the checks daily. A macOS watchdog runs outside
        GitHub, pins its installed verifier hashes, and reports state changes.
        Availability failures and integrity failures remain distinct, and expiry
        becomes a warning before it becomes an outage.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Offline recovery kit</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The sealed recovery kit contains signed Git bundles, current runbooks,
        public evidence, the deterministic identity archive, a Sigstore bundle,
        a trusted-root snapshot, and a checksum signature. It contains no secret
        key material. Exact repository commits and checksums make it usable when
        the Mac, GitHub, the public website, or a password-manager session is
        unavailable.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Operating principles</h2>

      <ol class="numberList">
        <li>
          Pin identities and workflow context, not only signature validity.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep routine release keys short-lived and keep recovery keys offline.
        </li>
        <li>State the exact guarantee of every check.</li>
        <li>
          Treat availability, integrity, freshness, and claim truth as different
          questions.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep audit evidence append-only while keeping normative documentation
          current.
        </li>
        <li>Never claim that an unperformed hardware ceremony is complete.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The <a href="/identity">identity page</a> links the public verifier, release
        archive, trust-bootstrap procedure, and recovery status.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
